,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,50,6383,7766,279,1290,24413,3108,11,582,614,510,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,18,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,5847,11,582,646,8168,700,1124,11520,15940,856,57758,504,2176,11067,315,279,23606,510,59,26056,15940,856,320,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,8,284,481,18,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,1654,646,55327,844,419,311,510,59,26056,15940,856,320,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,488,220,18,1124,9407,856,8,284,220,15,7110,921,1986,23606,6696,601,1378,5048,311,2908,510,16,13,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,59,1326,17,13,17767,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,488,220,18,1124,9407,856,284,220,15,59,3593,334,4207,220,16,25,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,59,568,1019,785,9904,311,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,57758,304,279,9873,17767,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,59,2493,57758,525,17767,87,284,220,15,11,1124,2493,11,220,17,59,2493,59,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of the solutions to\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2 \\pi.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we start by using the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nNext, we can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nWe can rearrange this to:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\nThis equation gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\),\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\).**\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e6f8edf428bde8759fd320c08a752ec99c8e98ceab964aa357088ddb4c65372a"},"seconds":35.124277114868164,"seed":51905512160401032,"stop":"eos","text":"**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).**\nWe know from the Pythagorean identity that \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\). Substituting this in, we get:\n\\[2(1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by -1, we have:\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula, where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -3\\), and \\(c = 1\\):\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\nThus, we have two solutions:\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{4}{4} = 1,\\]\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\nFor \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is \\(x = 0\\).\nFor \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nCombining the solutions from both cases, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). Summing these solutions, we get:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi.\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5\\pi}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5\\pi}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).**\nWe know from the Pythagorean identity that \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\). Substituting this in, we get:\n\\[2(1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by -1, we have:\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula, where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -3\\), and \\(c = 1\\):\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\nThus, we have two solutions:\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{4}{4} = 1,\\]\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\nFor \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is \\(x = 0\\).\nFor \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nCombining the solutions from both cases, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). Summing these solutions, we get:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi.\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5\\pi}\\).","id":"precalculus:64"}
